{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## Phase 2 — verification record (RED→GREEN, kill matrix, and the 128+N boundary)\n\n### The instrument lied first — worth recording\n\nThe FIRST kill-matrix run reported **all five mutations surviving**. That was not the oracle;\nit was the measuring harness. pytest colorizes its short summary, so the driver's\n`grep -c \"^FAILED\"` never matched — the line really begins with an ANSI escape, not `F`. The\nharness therefore reported `0 failed` **whether or not anything failed**.\n\nThat is precisely the defect under repair, one layer up: a check whose negative result is\nindistinguishable from \"the check never ran\". Re-run with `--color=no`, every mutation was\nkilled. Recording it because the failure mode is invisible in a clean-looking final matrix, and\nthe next person writing a mutation driver will reach for the same grep.\n\n### Kill matrix — 10 seeded, 10 killed, 0 survivors\n\nAxis A — re-introduce the confirmed root cause by deleting each site's guard:\n\n| mutation | RED |\n|---|---|\n| site 1 guard deleted | `test_git_floor_gate_skip_oracle_…` only |\n| site 2 guard deleted | `test_reconcile_routing_oracle_…` only |\n| site 3 guard deleted | `test_redispatch_no_warning_oracle_…` only |\n| site 4 guard deleted | `test_pandoc_colour_oracle_…` only |\n| site 5 guard deleted | `test_actionlint_fail_fast_oracle_…` only |\n\nOne-to-one, no cross-talk — the five tests are genuinely independent.\n\nAxis B — the OTHER independent sides, because a test that derives both sides of its assertion\nfrom one place measures that place, not the behaviour:\n\n| mutation | result |\n|---|---|\n| B1 shared predicate never fires (`< 0` → `< -9999`) | all 5 RED |\n| B2 shared message drops the signal name | all 5 RED (the message assertion is load-bearing) |\n| B3 shim stops killing (`exit 0`) | negative control + its 3 sites RED |\n| B5 bash-body kill side stops killing | sites 1 and 3 RED |\n| B4 shim exits **137** (positive) | sites do NOT catch it — the boundary, made executable |\n\n### The `128 + N` boundary — measured, not argued\n\nReviewer challenge: if a site's child is spawned through a shell, a real SIGKILL surfaces as\n`137` and never as `-9`, so accepting positive codes would leave that site unfixed while a test\nasserts it is a deliberate boundary. Checked empirically on each site's real spawn path rather\nthan reasoned from the call signature.\n\n**1. Which sites spawn directly?** All five. No `shell=True` anywhere in the five files; every\n`argv[0]` is the real program — `bash -c` (site 1), `sys.executable` (site 2), `bash <script>`\n(site 3), `pandoc` (site 4), `make` (site 5). Sites 1, 3 and 5 do have *grandchildren* (bash\nruns commands, make runs `sh`/`curl`); sites 2 and 4 have none.\n\n**2. `-9` or `137`?** Killing the process Python spawned yields **`-9` at all five**:\n\n```\nsite1  bash killed          rc=-9    output_empty=True\nsite1  grandchild killed    rc=-9    output_empty=True   (bash execs into it)\nsite3  bash killed          rc=-9    output_empty=True\nsite3  grandchild killed    rc=137   output_empty=False\nsite5  make killed          rc=-9    output_empty=True\nsite5  recipe child killed  rc=2     output_empty=False\nsite2  direct child killed  rc=-9    output_empty=True   (no grandchild)\nsite4  direct child killed  rc=-9    output_empty=True   (no grandchild)\n```\n\nNo site can *only* report `128 + N`, so none is left unfixed. The measurement also gives a\nbetter reason for the boundary than the one first offered. The original argument was \"rejecting\n137 would be a widening\". The stronger, measured claim: **an empty capture and a positive\nreturncode never co-occur here.** The fail-open needs an empty capture, and an empty capture at\nthese sites only ever arrives with a negative returncode, because whatever survives to report a\n`128 + N` also writes a diagnostic. When the code is positive the oracle has real output to\ninspect and is doing its job. This is now recorded in the regression test's docstring with the\ntable above.\n\n### Replication\n\nThe original report's own repro seam — patching `subprocess.run` globally, independent of the\nper-site seams the regression test uses — no longer reproduces at any of the five: each raises\n`AssertionError` naming SIGKILL and the site. 5 passed.\n\n### Residual risk\n\n- **Accepted:** the regression test forces each kill through a module-private seam\n  (`_gate_script`, `_redispatch_script`, `_pandoc_path`, `_SANE_PATH`, `sys.executable`). A\n  rename would break it without behaviour changing. Accepted because `monkeypatch.setattr`\n  raises `AttributeError` on a missing attribute — a rename fails LOUDLY rather than silently\n  passing, which is the correct direction for this bug class.\n- **Accepted:** the \"empty capture ⟹ negative returncode\" invariant above is measured and\n  documented, not asserted by a test. A future change that made a site's shell swallow its own\n  diagnostics would silently narrow the guard's coverage. Left as prose rather than adding\n  speculative future-proofing to test infrastructure.\n- **Observed, fixed in scope:** the five sites now use a bare `from _child_diag import …`, which\n  resolves only because the root `tests/conftest.py` puts `tests/` on `sys.path`. This is the\n  repo's existing pattern (`_dc_fixtures.py`, `test_dc_mutations.py`), so it was matched rather\n  than changed; it does mean these modules are not importable from outside `tests/`."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787312002625646001,"uuid":"9215812f-3ea5-4d41-a011-60f2865067b0"}